Malaria persists in the Brazilian Amazon despite decades of control, and how climate and
land use act on it remains hard to separate from the vector and human dynamics they
mediate. We analyse \textit{Plasmodium vivax} transmission in rural Manaus (2017-2023) and
show that the delayed SIR-SEI model of Parham and Michael (2010) cannot reproduce the
observed persistence, since recruitment cannot offset mosquito mortality. We reformulate it
as a climate-driven SEIRS-SEI model with density-dependent recruitment, humidity-dependent
adult survival and Bri\`ere-type sporogonic development, calibrated year by year, which
reproduces the observed inter- and intra-annual variability. The reproduction number
inherits the environmental state ($\mathcal{R}_0 = 2.20$, above unity on $39.8\%$ of
study-period days), so invasion risk varies within the year; local sensitivity attributes
it to mosquito survival and the biting rate, not to the human incubation period or immunity
waning. Releasing the per-bite probabilities weekly reduces the error in every year, so
constant coefficients cannot reproduce the seasonal structure; a random forest on
temperature, rainfall, humidity, deforestation and fire recovers it within years
($R^2 \approx 0.81-0.92$) but not between them ($R^2 \approx 0.17-0.19$).
Environmental drivers therefore act mainly by modulating time-varying per-bite transmission
probabilities, and because the parameters that decide threshold crossing differ from those
that set the wave height, interventions ranked from the reproduction number alone may be
misleading.
